  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The form had seventeen subsections. Shumei had filled out sixteen of them without difficulty—artifact designation, material composition, dimensional parameters, storage location, custodial lineage since the Schism. Only Section K remained: "Observable Magical Properties and Associated Risk Assessment."
    
    She noted this in the margin of her primary ledger with the careful notation style her mentor had taught her before the Schism: section completed, section pending, probable completion time. The probable completion time column read *forty minutes (standard assessment duration)*. This was optimistic. She added a secondary notation in the small book she kept for costs that didn't appear in primary accounts: *pending expert consultation; recommend caution*.
    
    She stood before R5-X6XN6VK9 in The Hearth Plaza's southern gallery, where the iron stand kept it away from the trade-floors but close enough for supervision. The crystalline lattice caught the stuttering aurora-light filtering down from the sealed upper levels. Beautiful, in the way dangerous things often were. The void at its center didn't catch anything.
    
    Shumei had inventoried worse. The Exchange's vaults held three fire-cores that still screamed if you listened at dawn, and that orca-carved bone-work Xouhoulong kept hidden had made her flippers ache just being near it. This was different—the cold here was specific, purposeful, like the ice itself was paying attention—but she'd managed the Aureate's founding records when they'd been half-burned and blood-stained. She could manage a piece of old aurora-glass.
    
    The checklist specified "direct assessment by qualified practitioner." Standard procedure. She documented her compliance with this requirement in the margin: *practitioner requested; arrival pending*.
    
    "You're certain," Shumei said, her voice carrying the measured cadence expected of Aurora Stack merchant-lines even when addressing a Nightshelf tunnel-scratcher who'd probably never seen proper daylight.
    
    The adept wore the ash-marks of Pactum∴myster across his beak, carved deep enough to scar. The ☽'visois symbols meant he could not speak his tenets without losing their power—a doctrine that made asking questions functionally useless. He'd introduced himself with a sound like ice cracking, probably not his real name, and had been staring at the Void Matrix for six full minutes without speaking.
    
    "I need verbal confirmation for the forms," Shumei prompted. "Observable properties. Documented risks. Standard assessment protocol."
    
    The adept's flippers moved in a complex gesture—down, spiral, sharp stop. Not words. He picked up a piece of frost-chalk and drew on the stone floor: the glacial Crystalline~ Spiral, except wrong, with gaps where the pattern should continue.
    
    "That's not helpful," Shumei said. "The Exchange requires—"
    
    He drew three more symbols she didn't recognize, then a simple pictogram: a penguin touching the matrix, a penguin with no light in its eyes, a penguin walking away but leaving its shadow behind.
    
    "So don't touch it. That's already documented. Keeper Shen's degradation was thoroughly recorded." She consulted her forms. "What I need is the specific mechanism of—"
    
    The adept made a sound in his throat that might have been a laugh or a death-rattle. He drew one final symbol—the ☽'visois Mask—and walked away. His footsteps echoed wrong in the plaza, too hollow, too far.
    
    Shumei looked at the chalk marks. The spiral with gaps. The pictogram of light draining away. Three other warnings she couldn't read because she'd never descended past the mining-guilds into absolute darkness where such things were taught.
    
    She made a note in her secondary ledger: *expert consultation complete; findings inconclusive; doctrine prohibits verbal communication of relevant details*. Then a supplementary notation: *self-classifying expertise; resolution unclear; recommend filing for later review*.
    
    She pulled out Subsection K-4: "When Expert Assessment Is Incomplete." There were procedures for this. Cross-reference with historical records, measure ambient aurora-density, document any observable changes to immediate environment. All very reasonable.
    
    The matrix hummed. Faint. A sound like aurora-light learning to sing in reverse.
    
    Shumei began taking measurements. She documented each one.
    
    ---
    
    The aurora-density gauge read negative. Which was impossible. Shumei tapped it twice, recalibrated against the known baseline readings from the upper galleries, and checked again. Still negative. As if the matrix wasn't just failing to emit light but was actively consuming it from the surrounding space.
    
    She wrote this down. Section K-4, Subsection ii: Observed Environmental Anomalies. Then in her secondary ledger: *quantity expected: positive; quantity observed: negative; variance: concerning*.
    
    The cold had intensified since she'd started. The kind that made your beak ache before you realized you were freezing. She'd been here half an hour—standard assessment duration per Exchange guidelines—and could no longer feel her toes. The temperature was precisely negative seventy-three and a half degrees, which was not correct. She documented the discrepancy.
    
    "Routine inventory," she muttered, flexing her flippers to restore circulation. "Standard procedure."
    
    The next form required "photographic documentation via ice-memory impression." She'd brought the necessary tools: a prepared frost-plate, an aurora-lamp for exposure, the calibrated lens-array. Simple archival work. She'd done this two hundred and forty-three times in the past season alone.
    
    She positioned the frost-plate on its stand, angled the lens toward the matrix, raised the aurora-lamp.
    
    The void at the center of R5-X6XN6VK9 didn't reflect the light. It swallowed it. The frost-plate went black in the center, not dark but *absent*, and the blackness began spreading outward through the crystalline structure like ink through water.
    
    Shumei lowered the lamp. The spreading stopped. The frost-plate was ruined—a perfect circle of nothing surrounded by fractured impressions of the lattice, all wrong, angles that hurt to look at.
    
    She documented this. Section K-4, Subsection v: Failed Archival Attempts and Material Loss. In the margin: *evidence consumed by artifact before review; please reconstruct from memory*. This was, she realized, becoming a filing problem.
    
    A junior merchant from the Prism-alignment stopped at the gallery entrance, drawn by the commotion or the cold. "Ledger-Warden? Is everything—"
    
    "Routine inventory," Shumei said. "Please maintain standard distance from artifacts under assessment."
    
    The junior merchant's glow was dimmer than it should be. They were standing thirty feet from the matrix and their aurora-light was already fading at the edges.
    
    Shumei checked the time. Forty-five minutes since she'd started. The plaza temperature had dropped eleven degrees. The mercury in her gauge had frozen solid. She made a note: *measurement instruments compromised; subsequent readings theoretical*.
    
    Section K-4, Subsection viii specified: "If anomalous effects spread beyond artifact's immediate vicinity, initiate containment protocols per Appendix D."
    
    Appendix D was sixty pages long. It began with "assemble qualified containment team of no fewer than five crystal-adepts" and progressed through increasingly complex ritual configurations that required, among other things, materials she didn't have, expertise she couldn't access without violating Pactum∴myster, and time she very clearly no longer possessed.
    
    The junior merchant's light went out entirely. They stood there, still breathing, but the glow was gone. Just... gone.
    
    "Go to the upper galleries," Shumei said. The formal cadence was slipping. "Now."
    
    They didn't move. Couldn't move? Wouldn't? The cold had them.
    
    Shumei grabbed their flipper—ice-cold through the ceremonial robes—and pulled. They stumbled forward, following mechanically, and she shoved them toward the exit. "Walk. Keep walking until you feel warm. Don't stop."
    
    She turned back to the matrix. The iron stand was frosting over. The stone floor beneath it had gone white. The void at its center seemed larger, though she knew that was impossible. Voids didn't grow. They were absences, not presences.
    
    But the absence was spreading.
    
    She documented this. The documentation felt increasingly inadequate. She documented that too.
    
    Three more merchants appeared at the gallery entrance, drawn by the cold or the rumors or the way light died near the southern section. Bursar Ingnao was with them, moving with the deliberate calm of someone who'd been called away from something important to deal with a crisis that definitely wasn't supposed to be happening.
    
    "Shumei. Report."
    
    "Routine inventory," she said. "Appendix D requires five crystal-adepts and materials not currently available. The artifact is exhibiting escalating anomalous properties in response to standard assessment procedures. I recommend—"
    
    The Hearth Plaza's aurora-light failed. All of it. Every carefully-aligned spire, every channeled beam, the entire cathedral of vertical crystals that had taken Xouhoulong years to design—dark. Not dimmed. Not flickering. Gone.
    
    In the sudden blackness, the Void Matrix was the only thing visible. It glowed. Not with light but with its opposite, a radiance of pure absence that made Shumei's eyes ache and her thoughts slow and her flippers shake with cold that came from somewhere far deeper than temperature.
    
    "What did you do," Ingnao said. Not a question. An accusation. A prayer. A plea.
    
    "I followed procedure," Shumei said. The words came out too slow, her beak too numb. "Section K. Observable properties. Risk assessment. Everything by the forms."
    
    Someone laughed. High and broken. Might have been her.
    
    The void kept growing. She kept documenting. These were, she understood, the same impulse.
    
    ---
    
    When the aurora-light finally returned—six hours later, after they'd physically relocated the matrix to the deepest storage vault, after Shumei had filled out emergency transfer forms with flippers that wouldn't stop shaking, after Bursar Ingnao had authorized the sealing of the entire southern gallery—it came back wrong.
    
    The spires still channeled sky-light down through the ice. The patterns still flowed according to Xouhoulong's original mathematics. But the rhythm stuttered now, irregular, like a heartbeat with a damaged valve. And the southern gallery stayed dark. The aurora wouldn't touch it. Refused to touch it, as if the ice itself remembered what had happened and wanted no part of it.
    
    Seven merchants had lost their glow during the cascade. Not dead—they still breathed, still moved, still went through the motions of their work—but the light was gone. Their aurora-magic, the birthright of every Stack penguin, simply... absent. Like the void had taken it for itself.
    
    Shumei added their names to her tertiary ledger—the small book she kept near the artifact for entries that shouldn't exist but did. Seven merchants' light, the records showed. Exactly seven. She documented each consumption with the same flat precision she applied to her crystal inventories. Someone had to.
    
    Bursar Ingnao read Shumei's documentation. All seventeen sections of it, plus the sixty pages of Appendix D she'd attempted to implement, plus the emergency protocols, plus the after-action assessment. Everything filled out correctly. Every procedure followed. Every requirement met.
    
    "This is thorough," Ingnao said, her voice carrying the exhaustion of someone who'd spent three days managing the aftermath of routine inventory. "Very thorough."
    
    "Thank you."
    
    "Don't thank me. I'm recommending we seal it permanently. No more assessments. No more procedures. We put it in the deep vault and we leave it there."
    
    "The Exchange will want regular monitoring. Custody requirements mandate—"
    
    "Shumei."
    
    She stopped. Looked up. Ingnao's eyes held something that might have been sympathy or might have been horror or might have been the particular resignation of someone who'd finally understood what Shumei had learned: that some things couldn't be managed, only documented.
    
    "File the sealing order," Ingnao said. "I'll handle the Exchange."
    
    ---
    
    Light-Regent Xouhoulong arrived on the eighth day.
    
    He moved with the careful stiffness of someone whose joints remembered cold that shouldn't have touched them, his formal robes immaculate, his circlet of aurora-glass catching light that the Hearth Plaza's damaged spires could no longer properly channel. Behind him, three aides carried documentation. Behind them, a delegation of Pactum∴myster adherents stood in perfect silence, their beaks marked with the ☽'visois symbols that meant they could not speak their tenets without losing their power.
    
    Shumei was completing her Post-Assessment Summary in the vault when he found her.
    
    "Ledger-Warden," Xouhoulong said. His voice held the measured dignity of a cracked crystal still expected to illuminate. "I have received reports of the cascade event. I require a full accounting."
    
    "Yes, Light-Regent." Shumei indicated her ledgers—primary, secondary, tertiary, the small book she kept near the artifact. "I have implemented comprehensive filing procedures."
    
    "I see." His gaze passed over the Void Matrix on its new iron stand and did not stop. No one's gaze stopped there. "And the political implications?"
    
    The ice beneath them shifted. Not physically—the vault's foundations were stable, mostly, if you ignored the corruption spreading through the repaired sections—but something in the memory-frost near Xouhoulong flickered. A frozen impression surfaced: a younger penguin's silhouette, ringed in light that wasn't aurora-glow.
    
    Shumei watched Xouhoulong not watch the ice remember him.
    
    "The Pactum∴myster delegation has filed a grievance," she said. "Their doctrine prohibits verbal communication of relevant details. Their written submission references 'costs that do not appear in ledgers' and requests 'remediation of the silence-breach that occurred when—'" She stopped. The next sentence had been redacted. By whom, the form did not specify. "The remainder is classified."
    
    "Classified by whom?"
    
    "By the act of reading it, apparently. The doctrine is self-enforcing."
    
    The Pactum∴myster adherents nodded. They could not explain further. Explaining would destroy the explanation. Shumei made a note: *self-classifying grievance; resolution unclear; recommend filing for later review*.
    
    An ancestor emerged from the wall behind Xouhoulong.
    
    The Long Dark was ending, but the veil was still thin enough. The figure wore the formal robes of a High-Beak Auditor. Translucent. Browsing. Watching.
    
    Shumei recognized the silhouette. So did Xouhoulong, by the way his flippers stilled against his sides.
    
    Selka. Frozen mid-reach toward a door that no longer existed. Preserved in the eastern galleries after the Calving tore the Berg's face open, three weeks after Xouhoulong's compact with the Midnight Claws, three days before his resignation. He had never visited her ice-memorial.
    
    The ancestor looked at him with the particular flatness of someone who has had eternity to consider what they might say, and has chosen not to say it.
    
    "Ledger-Warden," Xouhoulong said, his voice perfectly level. "Please add this manifestation to your filing queue."
    
    Shumei pulled out a fresh form. Claimant name. Date of death. Date of claim. Nature of grievance. Evidence provided.
    
    The evidence column remained empty. Selka simply stood near Xouhoulong for three hours, watching him not watch her, until the veil pulled her back into the ice-memory where she would wait until the next Long Dark made her solid enough to browse again.
    
    Her claim remained open. She had not filed anything. Some costs didn't appear in ledgers.
    
    ---
    
    The temperature in the vault was still negative seventy-three and a half degrees. The Void Matrix still hummed. No one asked why. The corruption in the repaired sections had spread four centimeters. Someone had submitted a maintenance request.
    
    Shumei stood before the sealed artifact, completing her final form: Post-Assessment Summary and Recommendations. She'd been putting it off for two days, but the deadline was here.
    
    Observable Magical Properties: Consumes aurora-light, dimensional presence expands under direct observation, affects sentient observers through proximity—
    
    She crossed it out. Started again.
    
    Observable Magical Properties: Don't touch it. Don't look at it. Don't try to measure it. Leave it alone.
    
    Associated Risk Assessment: Catastrophic.
    
    Recommendations: See attached sealing order.
    
    She signed it. Dated it. Added her merchant-seal.
    
    The Exchange had reviewed her documentation. Very pleased. Thorough, professional, properly formatted. They were recommending her for advanced custodial certification. Three more artifacts this season. High-priority items recovered during the Schism. They were confident she had the necessary expertise.
    
    Bursar Ingnao handed her the new forms. Section K was already highlighted.
    
    Shumei closed her primary ledger, then her secondary, then the small book she kept near the artifact for entries that shouldn't exist but did. She marked the final inventory count as *indeterminate* and added a note in the margin: *recommend continued documentation; costs that don't appear in ledgers remain unaccounted; next review: pending*.
    
    The aurora pulsed overhead, rhythm stuttering. The void hummed its reversed song. The sound was almost familiar now, almost comfortable. Like a warning you'd heard so many times it became lullaby.
    
    Shumei began to read.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771579002784_0", anchor: "that orca-carved bone-work Xouhoulong kept hidden", text: "Shumei drops this like a line item — one unpleasant artifact among several — and the chronicler lets her. But pause. Orca-carved bone-work. Not scavenged. Not seized. *Carved.* Bone-work implies tools, technique, aesthetic tradition. The orca are raiders in every official account, blood-priests howling hunt-songs at frequencies that crack ribs. They do not, in the approved narrative, produce *craft.* That Xouhoulong possessed such an object is interesting. That he kept it hidden is diagnostic. You do not hide trophies. You hide things that complicate the story you are telling about who the enemy is.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771579002784_1", anchor: "expertise she couldn't access without violating Pactum∴myster", text: "A Nightshelf adept, ash-marked and doctrine-bound, standing in an Aurora commercial gallery performing sanctioned assessment work. He arrived via the trade infrastructure the Flipper Accord maintains — the same machinery that moves fire-cores up and aurora-crystals down. The Accord can deliver a cross-cultural expert to your vault. It cannot make him talk. The entire Aurora-Nightshelf relationship, distilled into one silent penguin and one exasperated form.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579002784_2", anchor: "Exactly seven", text: "Exactly seven. The artifact's existing catalogue entry — which predates this assessment — already lists seven merchants consumed. The void, it seems, is a creature of precedent. Or someone recognized that the documentation was tidier if the new incident matched the old one, and stopped looking.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579002784_3", anchor: "By the act of reading it, apparently. The doctrine is self-enforcing.", text: "Exquisite. I have spent a career watching institutions fail to protect their records. Pactum∴myster has solved the problem by making the information destroy itself upon disclosure. The Foundation Depths should adopt this approach. It would save on filing.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579002784_4", anchor: "He had never visited her ice-memorial", text: "I wrote on the Hearth Plaza entry: 'Someone loved him. That is all this is.' The nesting signatures in the eastern gallery ice are not ambiguous. Three hours of a dead woman watching him not watch her, and he asks Shumei to *file* it. Of course he does.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579002784_5", anchor: "The temperature in the vault was still negative seventy-three and a half degrees", text: "Identical to her reading in the open southern gallery during the cascade — different room, different ventilation, different thermal mass, days later. That half-degree is performing the work of precision, but what it actually records is a fixed condition the artifact imposes on its surroundings regardless of context. Temperature as property of the object, not the environment. One other sealed case in the Foundation records exhibits this behavior. I mention this with the amusement of someone who will not be consulted.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771579002784_6", anchor: "The void kept growing. She kept documenting. These were, she understood, the same impulse.", text: "The best sentence in the collection. I do not say this lightly. Shumei has identified — mid-crisis, flippers numb, aurora dead — that documentation and consumption are the same gesture: the compulsion to make the unknown into something that fits in a ledger, even as the ledger becomes the mechanism of contact. She is, of course, rewarded with advanced custodial certification and three more artifacts. The Exchange recognizes excellence when it sees it. The void presumably agrees.", type: :commentary, display: :full
